t_adjustment": -0.03, "key_factors": ["Underwood's incumbency and massive fundraising advantage ($2.4M vs $141K)", "Unanimous expert consensus at Safe/Solid D", "D+3 district lean plus favorable 2026 environment", "Marter is a repeat underfunded challenger with no outside support"], "rationale": "The baseline model, all nine expert ratings, and Polymarket (95% D) all point to a comfortable Underwood win, reinforced by her dominant cash position and active district presence. No evidence justifies moving from the D+15 baseline.", "watch": "A late national environment shift toward Republicans or unexpected outside spending against Underwood could tighten the race, though not enough to threaten her.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Lauren Underwood (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Lauren Underwood seeks a fifth term in a district that leans Democratic by about three points, with the 2026 national environment adding to her advantage. She has raised over $2.4 million and holds more than $1.1 million on hand, while Republican James Marter has raised roughly $141,000 with no outside support on either side. Underwood has been highly visible, announcing federal funding for district projects and holding town halls across the district.\n\nEvery major expert rating places the race at Safe or Solid Democratic, and prediction markets give her about a 95 percent chance of winning. With no public polling available, the forecast rests on fundamentals, ratings, and money, all of which align. Marter ran a low-budget campaign focused on healthcare and spending, but the resource gap and incumbent strength make an upset highly unlikely.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9854, "margin": 15.03}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9812, "margin": 15.0}]}